WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.739
Wait, Quentin Tarantino almost made a "Star
Trek" movie?

00:00:03.739 --> 00:00:05.279
And it was going to be R rated?

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:07.750
Like "'Pulp Fiction' in space"?

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:08.910
Yes.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.730
Keep watching for the details about what could
have been.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:17.110
If Quentin Tarantino doesn't strike you as
the kind of director Paramount would have

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:20.210
had on its shortlist for "Star Trek," you'd
be right.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:25.960
The humble beginnings of Tarantino's never-made
sci-fi blockbuster came from Tarantino himself,

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.670
not the studio.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:32.420
Tarantino is an admitted fan of the franchise
who has cited the original series' "The City

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:37.860
On The Edge of Forever" and the "Next Generation"
episode "Yesterday's Enterprise" as personal

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:38.860
favorites.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:45.370
"One more ship will make no difference in
the here and now, but 22 years ago, one ship

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:48.431
could have stopped this war before it started."

00:00:48.431 --> 00:00:53.050
It began with a 2015 interview with Chris
Hardwick, in which Tarantino discussed his

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:56.950
love of "Trek" and his potential interest
in helming a movie.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:02.210
Hardwick initially asked the director about
"Star Wars," but Tarantino pivoted to say:

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.920
"I would be more inclined to do a 'Star Trek'
kind of thing."

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:11.710
In September 2017, TMZ cornered Tarantino
at an airport and questioned whether he'd

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:13.970
actually direct such a project.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:14.970
He responded,

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.190
"It would be worth having a meeting."

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:21.972
By December of that year, Deadline reported
he was officially on board, and a writer's

00:01:21.972 --> 00:01:25.600
room was being assembled to flesh out his
pitch.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.280
Things moved quickly, and just a few short
weeks after the writer's room was put together,

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:35.870
Deadline reported that Mark L. Smith was announced
as the writer of the proposed film.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:40.350
Smith's previous work, which included the
Leonardo DiCaprio drama "The Revenant," was

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:46.930
a far cry from the likes of previous "Trek"
writers Roberto Orci and Alex Kurtzman, mostly

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:52.190
know for pennig popcorn blockbusters such
as "Transformers" and "Cowboys &amp; Aliens."

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:56.550
In an interview with Bulletproof Screenwriting,
Smith spoke about writing the script with

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.830
Tarantino and working with J.J.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.810
Abrams' production company Bad Robot.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:01.810
He said:

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:02.810
"They just called me.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.071
It was a like a day later, and said, 'Hey,
are you up for it?

00:02:05.071 --> 00:02:06.071
Do you want to go?

00:02:06.071 --> 00:02:09.080
And if so, Quentin wants to hook up.'

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.520
So I said, yeah, sure."

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.520
He continued:

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.020
" And that was the first day I met Quentin,
in the room and he's reading a scene that

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.020
he wrote.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.980
I told him, I was so mad I didn't record it
on my phone.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.180
It would be so valuable.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.860
It was amazing."

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:28.240
Smith gave a few hints about the still-unknown
plot, but mostly talked about his enthusiasm

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:29.880
about the project.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:34.980
In a 2019 interview with Empire Magazine,
Tarantino himself noted that the script was

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.680
done as he weighed his options for his next
project, saying,

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:41.170
"There's a script that exists for it now.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:44.630
I need to weigh in on it, but I haven't been
able to do that yet."

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:50.700
A year after the project's announcement, the
film's story was still a tightly guarded secret.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.290
Nobody knew who would star.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:56.900
But the biggest question on everybody's lips
was whether Tarantino himself would direct

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.830
or just take producer and story credits.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:04.520
Paramount must have been hopeful he would
take the helm, but many outlets were skeptical,

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:09.110
mostly because Tarantino wasn't known for
directing properties that he hadn't created.

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:14.560
While he had directed a handful of episodes
for TV series including "ER" and "CSI," his

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:19.060
films were all originals, with the exception
of "Jackie Brown," which was an adaptation

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.849
of an Elmore Leonard novel.

00:03:20.849 --> 00:03:25.739
Two years into development, Tarantino was
still uncertain whether he'd direct, and the

00:03:25.739 --> 00:03:28.959
internet was abuzz with rumors that he might
be stepping away.

00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:31.709
Deadline caught up with him and asked him
about the gossip.

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:32.709
He replied,

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:35.220
"I might be steering away from it, but we'll
see.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.180
I haven't completely decided or talked to
anyone involved.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:39.500
Nothing is official."

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:44.480
In 2020, Deadline reported Tarantino had officially
dropped out, and that if the movie were to

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.620
be made, he wouldn't be sitting in the director's
chair.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:52.769
Tarantino's ninth film as director, "Once
Upon a Time ... in Hollywood," hit theaters

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:54.310
in 2019.

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:59.670
And while that movie made quite a splash,
his "Star Trek" proposal was still a big topic.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:03.640
Speaking once again to Deadline, the director
revealed that his "Trek" would be a balance

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:07.510
between classic "Star Trek" and his own "Pulp
Fiction."

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:12.220
Tarantino also expressed his grievances with
"Trek" actors who had tried to tamp down the

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:13.220
project.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:17.669
He said their comments weren't accurate because
they weren't as in-the-know as they claimed.

00:04:17.669 --> 00:04:20.329
He named one name specifically, saying:

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:22.440
"I get annoyed at Simon Pegg.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.759
He doesn't know anything about what's going
on, and he keeps making all these comments

00:04:26.759 --> 00:04:28.900
as if he knows about stuff.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:32.229
One of the comments he said, he's like 'Well,
look, it's not going to be 'Pulp Fiction'

00:04:32.229 --> 00:04:33.229
in space.'

00:04:33.229 --> 00:04:34.229
Yes, it is!

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:36.550
[...] It's, at the very least, unique in that
regard."

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:39.069
"That's an interesting point.

00:04:39.069 --> 00:04:42.870
Come on, let's get into character."

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.849
One actor who didn't seem to upset Tarantino
with his comments was Karl Urban, who played

00:04:47.849 --> 00:04:51.009
Leonard "Bones" McCoy in the three J.J.

00:04:51.009 --> 00:04:53.099
Abrams-produced "Star Trek" films.

00:04:53.099 --> 00:04:58.270
In a 2018 interview with TrekMovie.com, the
actor said he heard the pitch and confirmed

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:00.180
just how wild it was.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:05.659
He also confirmed rumors that the film would
carry an R rating, a first for the franchise.

00:05:05.659 --> 00:05:10.189
While some were concerned this meant the film
would be filled with cursing and nudity, Urban

00:05:10.189 --> 00:05:14.930
noted that it was more about showcasing the
true horrors of space, explaining:

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:18.560
"You shouldn't worry that it is going to be
full of obscenity and stuff.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.409
He wants an R rating to really make those
beats of consequence land.

00:05:22.409 --> 00:05:25.659
... It allows some breadth ... gives him some
leeway to do that."

00:05:25.659 --> 00:05:31.039
According to Deadline, Tarantino himself had
asked the studio for an R rating, with Paramount

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:33.909
and production partner Bad Robot agreeing
to it.

00:05:33.909 --> 00:05:38.430
The director later confirmed it in his 2019
Deadline interview, saying,

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:44.509
"If you've seen my nine movies, you kind of
know my way is an R-rated way and a way that

00:05:44.509 --> 00:05:46.979
is without certain restrictions."

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:49.759
Tarantino also said that producer J.J.

00:05:49.759 --> 00:05:54.559
Abrams was fully onboard and likened it to
recent R-rated blockbusters, saying,

00:05:54.559 --> 00:05:58.289
"'Deadpool' showed that you can rethink these
things, do them in a different way.

00:05:58.289 --> 00:06:00.099
So really, even before J.J.

00:06:00.099 --> 00:06:05.479
knew what the idea was, his feeling was, if
it wants to be an R rating, fine.

00:06:05.479 --> 00:06:09.629
If it wants to be the 'Wild Bunch' in space,
fine."

00:06:09.629 --> 00:06:14.589
Without plot details, many wondered if we
might see Chris Pine and Zachary Quinto return,

00:06:14.589 --> 00:06:19.159
especially given that the last film in the
series, "Star Trek Beyond," was considered

00:06:19.159 --> 00:06:20.580
a disappointment.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.909
Some wondered if there might be an all new
cast.

00:06:22.909 --> 00:06:27.990
But Tarantino himself professed his love for
the Abrams cast, and it soon became clear

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:31.889
that if the film were to move forward, that
cast would return.

00:06:31.889 --> 00:06:35.030
In the 2017 Deadline interview, Tarantino
said,

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:38.749
"That's why I actually really was so taken
with J.J.

00:06:38.749 --> 00:06:43.559
Abrams' first reboot and how fantastic I thought
Chris Pine channeled William Shatner.

00:06:43.559 --> 00:06:48.169
... He's playing the William Shatner version
of Kirk, and he's doing a fantastic job at

00:06:48.169 --> 00:06:49.169
it.

00:06:49.169 --> 00:06:50.849
I mean perfect, frankly.

00:06:50.849 --> 00:06:53.599
And [Zachary] Quinto is perfect as Spock."

00:06:53.599 --> 00:06:59.279
In July 2018, Quinto spoke to USA Today and
confirmed his involvement alongside the cast

00:06:59.279 --> 00:07:01.120
of the previous three films.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:05.389
"I'm thrilled that we might have the opportunity
to work with him and see what he would do

00:07:05.389 --> 00:07:06.959
with us in this universe.

00:07:06.959 --> 00:07:12.080
... I've been a fan of his for years, and
I'm really inspired by his originality."

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:17.240
In his interview with Bulletproof Screenwriter,
Mark L. Smith explained how the story of the

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.999
movie involved a "cool gangster scene."

00:07:19.999 --> 00:07:25.509
A Deadline report also mentioned that it might
be a 1930s gangster story, which immediately

00:07:25.509 --> 00:07:31.030
got fans speculating that it could be inspired
by or related to the classic original series

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:34.020
episode "A Piece of the Action."

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:39.289
The 1968 episode of the original "Star Trek"
series saw Kirk, Spock and Bones land on an

00:07:39.289 --> 00:07:44.839
alien planet with a culture entirely based
around Prohibition-era Chicago.

00:07:44.839 --> 00:07:49.659
The planet had apparently been visited by
another Federation starship in the days before

00:07:49.659 --> 00:07:55.199
the Prime Directive, and that ship unwittingly
contaminated the culture by leaving behind

00:07:55.199 --> 00:07:58.050
a book titled "Chicago Mobs Of the Twenties."

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:04.149
Though it may not be what many expected, a
"Star Trek" gangster film certainly fit Tarantino's

00:08:04.149 --> 00:08:09.460
description of something we'd never seen in
a big sci-fi movie, and there's no doubt it

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:14.780
would have offered him plenty of opportunities
for the kinds of crime stories he does best.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:19.009
Following the release of "Star Trek Beyond,"
Paramount was unsure of where to take the

00:08:19.009 --> 00:08:20.069
franchise.

00:08:20.069 --> 00:08:24.809
While audiences and critics seemed to like
the film, it disappointed at the ticket counter,

00:08:24.809 --> 00:08:27.689
failing to live up to its predecessors.

00:08:27.689 --> 00:08:32.180
Unsure of how to proceed, Paramount was thrilled
to hear of Tarantino's interest.

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:36.940
But the studio also decided not to put all
its eggs in one basket.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:41.620
Spock actor Zachary Quinto told Deadline,
there were as many as three different scripts

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:45.320
in development before the sequel was officially
given the green light.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:50.440
"I think there's a couple of scripts, you
know, because there was a script being written

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.590
before Quentin Tarantino."

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:57.880
Quinto told ET Canada that a sequel written
by Simon Pegg and Doug Jung was in the works

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:01.490
before Tarantino approached the studio in
2017.

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:06.720
Noah Hawley of "Fargo" and "Legion" was also
reportedly developing a screenplay that nearly

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.370
went into production.

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:09.370
Screenwriters J.D.

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:14.750
Payne and Patrick McKay were also working
on a story that was dubbed "Star Trek 4."

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:17.180
The project was said to be directed by S.J.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:22.130
Clarkson and would have starred Chris Hemsworth,
apparently entirely separate from the version

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:26.940
entering production in 2022 to be directed
by Matt Shakman.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:31.550
Rod Roddenberry, son of "Star Trek" creator
Gene Roddenberry, was pretty vocal about his

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:36.490
displeasure with Paramount bringing in Tarantino
to helm a new "Trek" film.

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:41.580
Roddenberry serves as an executive producer
on the many new "Star Trek" series, including

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:45.870
"Discovery" and "Strange New Worlds," and
he also acts as a steward for the brand his

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:46.920
father created.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:52.960
In a 2021 interview with Forbes, he said he's
a fan of Tarantino, insisting that his rejection

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:57.210
of an R-rated Tarantino "Trek" film had to
do with the integrity of the franchise.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:58.380
He said:

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:03.020
"I struggle with [a Tarantino "Star Trek"
film] because the way I'm pretty myopic with

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:04.590
the way I see 'Star Trek.'

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:08.871
I grew up with fans [...] saying how 'Star
Trek' inspired them and gave them hope for

00:10:08.871 --> 00:10:10.020
the future.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:14.500
It's the optimism and the messaging in there
that make 'Star Trek' what it was."

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:19.280
With that in mind, Roddenberry didn't love
the idea of "Pulp Fiction" in space.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:20.280
He continued:

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:24.811
"I'll be honest, that doesn't work for me,
but he is a fan, and I think as a fan, he

00:10:24.811 --> 00:10:31.130
probably understands to some degree that 'Star
Trek' has to have some of this [hopeful, optimistic]

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:32.180
messaging."

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:37.080
Many "Trek" fans were excited about the prospect
of Tarantino producing something risky and

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:38.080
daring.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:40.540
One user on Trekmovie.com wrote,

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:45.430
"Tarantino would bring something fresh and
the characters would be vibrant and logical.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:49.750
They added that the director's involvement
would "reinvigorate the film franchise."

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:51.990
Others weren't as happy about it.

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:57.420
Another user declared that "under no circumstances
should this be allowed to happen."

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:01.780
On Reddit, user DylanVincent seemed to agree
with Rod Roddenberry, saying,

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:03.810
"This is such a bad idea.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:07.730
The original spirit of 'Star Trek' gets no
respect anymore."

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:10.850
Another Redditor, Conchobair, offered another
viewpoint, saying:

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:12.650
"I really hope this happens.

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:13.850
This would be insane.

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:18.360
I love 'Trek' and Tarantino, and they [...] together
could be amazing."

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:23.740
With the idea reportedly scrapped, it seems
the naysayers got their way.

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:29.070
As the rumor mill swirled about Tarantino's
"Star Trek" project, several major "Star Trek"

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:32.660
alumni expressed interest in reprising their
roles.

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:37.090
In an interview with Reuters, William Shatner
expressed his love for the director and his

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:39.960
passionate longing to return as Captain Kirk.

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:41.180
He said:

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:45.660
"Quentin Tarantino said something in the paper
that somebody said to me, that he loved me.

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:47.560
And Quentin, I love you.

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:52.250
If you're going to do 'Star Trek,' hey, 50
years later, a few pounds heavier, come on,

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:53.430
not a problem.

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:54.620
... I'd like to do it."

00:11:54.620 --> 00:12:00.500
George Takei, who played Sulu on the original
series, told Yahoo! Entertainment of his interest,

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:03.850
but also said it would all depend on the script.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:07.380
And it wasn't just original series stars who
spoke up.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:11.550
Patrick Stewart, who played Captain Picard
on "Star Trek: The Next Generation" made it

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:16.640
clear that he'd love to return if Tarantino
was involved, telling The Hollywood Reporter,

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:19.600
"One of my dreams is to work with Tarantino.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:24.750
I admire his work so much, and to be in a
Tarantino film would give me so much satisfaction.

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:29.560
So, if he is going to direct something to
do with 'Star Trek' [...] I would embrace

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:30.560
it."

